      "quote": "Would a proposed amendment widen the scope of the specification as filed? If so it will not be allowed under section 65 - see paragraph 4.8."
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Under the Manual’s practice, the concern is a goods list that is unrealistically broad in commercial terms—not merely a long list. [TMM/Part14/6/6/6]\n\nFor that concern, the Manual says you should be asked to do one of two things:\n• Confirm by declaration that you intend to use the trade mark on all the goods specified; or\n• Propose a narrower goods list, limited to the actual goods on which the trade mark is used or intended to be used. [TMM/Part14/6/6/6]\n\nThe Manual also says a proposed amendment cannot widen the scope of the specification as filed, referring to section 65. This is a statement of Manual practice; section 65 itself has not been supplied. [TMM/Part14/4/4/4]\n\nThe Regulations provide the statutory declaration power: if the Registrar reasonably suspects that paragraph 27(1)(b) of the Act does not apply to all specified goods or services, the Registrar may require a declaration that it applies to all of them. Paragraph 27(1)(b) has not been supplied, so its full requirements cannot be explained from these passages. [TMR1995/r4.8(3)]"
